blocks.body:
Swapping or regrouping a subtraction or a division changes the answer:

- $7 - 3 = 4$, but $3 - 7 = -4$;
- $(10 - 4) - 2 = 4$, but $10 - (4 - 2) = 8$;
- $12 \div 4 = 3$, but $4 \div 12 = \frac{1}{3}$;
- $(16 \div 4) \div 2 = 2$, but $16 \div (4 \div 2) = 8$.

So mental methods that reorder or regroup, which are safe for sums and products, give wrong answers when applied to differences and quotients as they stand. The section on inverses shows how to rewrite them so that the laws apply.
